  <h1 id="top">Righteous Felon Jerky: An Honest Review from a Pitmaster</h1>

  <div class="answer-box">
    <p><strong>Righteous Felon</strong> is a craft beef jerky brand founded in 2011 in Chester County, Pennsylvania, known for bold outlaw-themed flavors like OG Hickory, Habanero Escobar, and Bourbon Franklin. Made from pasture-raised American black angus with no added hormones or preservatives, it sits firmly in the premium tier at roughly $3.50 per ounce. <strong>My rating: 4.0/5.</strong></p>
  </div>

  <ContentImage src="/images/righteous-felon-jerky-hero.webp" alt="A variety of Righteous Felon craft beef jerky bags on a wooden kitchen counter with jerky pieces spilling from one open package" eager={true} />

  <p>I make beef jerky for a living, and Righteous Felon is one of the small handful of craft brands I actively pay retail for. That is a real endorsement — most jerky I get free from the industry, and most of it goes in the trash after one tasting. <em>[Switches to serious face]</em> RF isn't perfect. The branding leans heavily on outlaw-cartel-Escobar pastiche which some readers will find charming and others will find try-hard. The price runs premium ($6.99 for a 2 oz bag is roughly triple what a Jack Link's costs per ounce). And their distribution is inconsistent enough that your favorite flavor might be sold out when you order. But the actual jerky is genuinely good, the sourcing standards are real, and the flavor lineup is more interesting than 90% of what sits on grocery shelves. Full honest review — every flavor, the brand story, where to buy, and my verdict at the end.</p>

  <nav class="toc" aria-label="Table of contents">
    <p class="toc__title">In this review</p>
    <ol>
      <li><a href="#righteous-felon-og-hickory-beef-jerky">Righteous Felon OG Hickory Beef Jerky</a></li>
